A||||10||Gokart||Back grill in middle instead of two sides??
Z||000000||Gokart||05-22-99||05:05 AM||||I saw some pictures on line of Fieros with a scoop in the middle! Did some models come with one grill in the middle instead of two side ones or is this an aftermarket job?
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-22-99||07:17 AM||||The '84 Fieros had a grille in the middle of the rear deck lid; the removable panels on either side were solid.  When Pontiac added the V6 in 1985, they removed the grille and extended the deck lid with a hump to clear the engine, and made the removable panels on either side into grilles, which is the case on all 1985-88 Fieros.<p>Incidentally, the decklid grille on the 1984 Fiero was made of some sort of exotic lightweight metal (titanium, maybe?)  It was really brittle...the one on my old 84 (God rest her soul) was split on either side, resulting in a cheap-ass sounding "clangggg" when you shut the decklid.
Z||000002||Gokart||05-22-99||10:01 AM||||My guess is it wouldn't be a great idea for a scoop for an 84 seeing it could fall through to the engine!!!
Z||000003||theogre||05-22-99||03:06 PM||||Personally I'm glad they changed the deck setup.... Those solid panels always look cruddy.<p>I'm getting an 84 soon. I have an extra set of the slotted pannels, maybe I'll stick them on and see how they look. Sure would improve heat escape having it vented all the way across.
Z||000004||PaUL||05-24-99||01:50 PM||||The rear grill in the 84s were made out of a magnesium alloy. The fiero received some  kind of award for this (most creative use of magnesium or something like that). The 2 smaller side grills on the 85-88s were also made out of magnesium alloy. It is kind of brittle, mine is cracked on the one that goes over the battery. 
Z||000005||Racer 1||06-02-99||02:54 PM||||both the 84-85's coupes had those ugly single middle vent then in 86 they came out with the hump back which is way better looking! but if you have a 86 take off the metal grills that go under the two side vent !!!! 
Z||000006||BOILERMAKER||06-02-99||03:43 PM||||I'd check on your facts again before you go any further with the 85 having the middle vent.
Z||000007||Carrolles||06-03-99||08:25 AM||||The center grill was only on the 84.<p>I wouldn't suggest removing the screen material from under the side grills since their purpose is to stop leaves and other junk from getting into the engine bay.<p>Also I have been curious as to what benefit the over the roof scoops are on the 85-88s since the natural airflow through the grills is upward.  Seems like the scoops push the air in the wrong direction.
Z||000008||theogre||06-03-99||12:32 PM||||There are 2 types of grates used in the corners of the rear deck.<p>if your "old" style grills have the "hex mesh" (the holes are hexagonal, not the screen) that most of the pre 87 grills I've seen have, there is no benifit to removing it. the restriction it presents is so small it's basically not there. On this version the shape of the louvers is the restriction. Short of altering each louver, there's not much you could do to improve them.<p>If you have the "new" style grill with the stamped sheet of slots under them then removing the sheet would open them up allot. Those slots are pretty small.<p>The reason they went to the new grate is that it uses less magnesium or whatever they are made of that costs allot. Remember that even a few cent savings per part when you make 100,000 of them is a huge saving. The problem was that now you can see everything under them. They changed to the stamped slot grate to hide the engine compartment.<p>One way you could fix them, other than just ripping out the slot sheet, is to take the hex screen from a trashed old style grate and put it under the new ones. That would trap the big trash, but still let air flow free. You end up with a grate that looks close to stock when you are done.
Z||000009||Eric||06-03-99||02:20 PM||||Ogre, I took my "new style" grill sheets out to refinish them.  I haven't got around to popping them back in yet because I was trying to figure out a better solution.  It's like you said, the sheets really restrict the amount of cool air that gets in and the hot air that gets out.  I don't have access to an old car, so it might be better for me to think of a substitute for the grill backing.  I would like to get some material that cuts down on the amount of water that's allowed in because every time after a good shower, when I start my car, the belts squeal.
Z||000010||Motorhead||06-03-99||06:25 PM||||Hey ya'll, how 'bout RS Motorsports' scoop!  Looks like a great way to improve airflow through the engine compartment.<p>More of a vent than a scoop, but I like the looks of it. it even keeps te air moving in the "correct" direction!<p>Anybody got one?
